  "new_figures": [
    {
      "id": "choice-graph-pair-a",
      "path": ".tmp/resume182-4-postaccount/n13-choice-graphs-FIRST/choice-graph-pair-a-label-repair1.svg",
      "sha256": "771d999f812a1caff1784b753d3df2481108253d44a987dc2542e2db193fbf51",
      "basis": "Correct pair; both panels use y=6/x with its two branches. 6/x=x-1 -> x^2-x-6=0 -> x=-2,3. 6/x=x+3 -> x^2+3x-6=0 -> x=(-3+-sqrt(33))/2. All roots are nonzero and determine the listed y coordinates. Common x[-6,6], y[-8,8] unit grids; panel I then II, no curve-equation labels. Noninteger intersection abscissas rounded to three decimals, integer abscissas exact. Reciprocal branches use equation-derived segment approximation below 0.04 px error at the 640 px display width; exact roots are knots. Crop limits do not change x!=0 or declare domain endpoints."
    },
    {
      "id": "choice-graph-pair-b",
      "path": ".tmp/resume182-4-postaccount/n13-choice-graphs-FIRST/choice-graph-pair-b-FIRST.svg",
      "sha256": "0548ae86103c2056b259ef5808bac394b41af32fc4d6578147ebe0f0e17bad76",
      "basis": "Deliberately replace the reciprocal curve y=6/x with y=6x in both panels; keep the original lines. Misread x^-1 as x, giving 6x=x-1 -> 5x=-1 and 6x=x+3 -> 5x=3. Coordinates follow y=6x. This is a reproducible wrong graphical construction, not a fabricated source fact. Common x[-6,6], y[-8,8] unit grids; panel I then II, no curve-equation labels. Noninteger intersection abscissas rounded to three decimals, integer abscissas exact. Reciprocal branches use equation-derived segment approximation below 0.04 px error at the 640 px display width; exact roots are knots. Crop limits do not change x!=0 or declare domain endpoints."
    },
    {
      "id": "choice-graph-pair-c",
      "path": ".tmp/resume182-4-postaccount/n13-choice-graphs-FIRST/choice-graph-pair-c-FIRST.svg",
      "sha256": "92f1944b7968d88e18ebddfcd09698cf159e59747ff05bc4b865ea3526b980eb",
      "basis": "Keep y=6/x correct, but reverse the signs of both vertical shifts: panel I uses y=x+1 and panel II uses y=x-3. Reversing line-shift directions gives 6/x=x+1 -> x^2+x-6=0 -> x=-3,2, and 6/x=x-3 -> x^2-3x-6=0 -> x=(3+-sqrt(33))/2. This preserves the reciprocal curve while exposing a precise shift-sign misconception. Common x[-6,6], y[-8,8] unit grids; panel I then II, no curve-equation labels. Noninteger intersection abscissas rounded to three decimals, integer abscissas exact. Reciprocal branches use equation-derived segment approximation below 0.04 px error at the 640 px display width; exact roots are knots. Crop limits do not change x!=0 or declare domain endpoints."
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "normalize-audit",
      "source_numbers": [
        "168"
      ],
      "view": {
        "mode": "single",
        "stem": "标准形式为 $a\\cdot10^n$，其中 $1\\leq a<10$、$n\\in\\mathbb Z$，数量级是指数 $n$。依次考察 $3.4\\cdot10^8$、$7.51\\cdot10^5$、$10.6\\cdot10^9$、$5.2\\cdot10^{-4}$、$19\\cdot10^{-6}$、$0.4\\cdot10^{-8}$。哪张表同时正确判断了原式是否已是标准形式，给出了各数的标准形式和数量级？各表行序与上述数的顺序一致。",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "text": "| 原式已标准 | 标准形式 | 数量级 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 是 | $3.4\\cdot10^8$ | $8$ |\n| 是 | $7.51\\cdot10^5$ | $5$ |\n| 是 | $10.6\\cdot10^9$ | $9$ |\n| 是 | $5.2\\cdot10^{-4}$ | $-4$ |\n| 是 | $19\\cdot10^{-6}$ | $-6$ |\n| 是 | $0.4\\cdot10^{-8}$ | $-8$ |",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "text": "| 原式已标准 | 标准形式 | 数量级 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 是 | $3.4\\cdot10^8$ | $8$ |\n| 是 | $7.51\\cdot10^5$ | $5$ |\n| 否 | $1.06\\cdot10^{10}$ | $10$ |\n| 是 | $5.2\\cdot10^{-4}$ | $-4$ |\n| 否 | $1.9\\cdot10^{-5}$ | $-5$ |\n| 否 | $4\\cdot10^{-9}$ | $-9$ |",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "text": "| 原式已标准 | 标准形式 | 数量级 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 是 | $3.4\\cdot10^8$ | $8$ |\n| 是 | $7.51\\cdot10^5$ | $5$ |\n| 否 | $1.06\\cdot10^8$ | $8$ |\n| 是 | $5.2\\cdot10^{-4}$ | $-4$ |\n| 否 | $1.9\\cdot10^{-7}$ | $-7$ |\n| 否 | $4\\cdot10^{-7}$ | $-7$ |",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Classify and normalize the complete six-expression collection, including coefficients above and below the permitted interval, and report every quantity order.",
        "source_solution": "Already standard: 3.4*10^8, 7.51*10^5, 5.2*10^-4. Normalize 10.6*10^9 to 1.06*10^10; 19*10^-6 to 1.9*10^-5; 0.4*10^-8 to 4*10^-9. Orders in source order: 8,5,10,-4,-5,-9.",
        "unit_reason": "The mathematical task is a complete audit under one coefficient-interval definition, not six separate classification clicks. Each option is the whole audit.",
        "concept_blocks": [
          3
        ],
        "limitations": [
          "Recognizing the complete table does not demonstrate independently writing the normalized expressions."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "b"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "a",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Applying the erroneous interval 0.1<=a<100 accepts all six original coefficients. Leaving every expression unchanged gives orders 8,5,9,-4,-6,-8 and the all-yes table.",
            "misconception": "Replaces 1<=a<10 with 0.1<=a<100 and therefore never normalizes 10.6,19 or 0.4."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "b",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "Dividing coefficients 10.6 and 19 by 10 increases their exponents to 10 and -5; multiplying coefficient 0.4 by 10 decreases its exponent to -9. The other three already lie in [1,10). This yields exactly the six rows.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "c",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Correctly identify the three coefficients needing adjustment, but move the exponent in the same direction as the coefficient: 10.6/10 with 9-1=8; 19/10 with -6-1=-7; 0.4*10 with -8+1=-7. Preserve the already-standard rows.",
            "misconception": "Fails to compensate a coefficient change by an opposite change in the power of ten."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
